Yes, I am familiar with Richard Byles — he is the Governor of the Bank of Jamaica (the central bank). He was appointed in August 2019. Before becoming Governor, he had a long career in business and finance, including leadership roles like co-chair of the Economic Programme Oversight Committee (EPOC), and he was also CEO of Sagicor Group Jamaica for many years.

As Governor, he’s been focused on modernizing the Bank of Jamaica, especially moving it towards a full inflation-targeting framework and greater monetary policy independence (especially after Jamaica’s IMF program ended). Under his leadership, the Bank of Jamaica has also introduced digital innovations like Jamaica’s central bank digital currency (CBDC), called Jam-Dex.

Were you looking for something specific about him — like his policies, leadership style, recent speeches, or something else?

Governor Richard Byles of the Bank of Jamaica (BOJ) has recently delivered several speeches and overseen key policy initiatives aimed at maintaining economic stability and fostering growth. Below is a summary of his notable addresses and policy actions over the past year:

1. Quarterly Monetary Policy Report Press Conference – February 24, 2025
	•	Policy Decision: The Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) unanimously agreed to maintain the policy rate at 6.0% and to preserve relative stability in the foreign exchange market. This decision was based on favorable macroeconomic data and forecasts, with headline inflation at 4.7% in January 2025, down from 7.4% in January 2024.  ￼

2. Macroeconometric Caribbean Conference – March 20, 2025
	•	Speech: Governor Byles presented on “Managing the Post-COVID Inflation Shock,” discussing Jamaica’s experience with inflation targeting during recent global economic challenges. He highlighted the country’s proactive monetary policies and the importance of maintaining price stability.  ￼

3. Quarterly Monetary Policy Report Press Conference – November 25, 2024
	•	Policy Decision: The MPC decided to lower the policy rate by 25 basis points to 6.25%, effective November 22, 2024. This decision reflected the committee’s view that inflation was becoming more anchored within the BOJ’s target range of 4.0% to 6.0%, with headline inflation at 4.9% in October 2024.  ￼

4. Standing Finance Committee of Parliament – July 23, 2024
	•	Statement: Governor Byles discussed the BOJ’s conduct of monetary policy, noting the decision to start a gradual easing of the monetary policy stance. This involved reducing the BOJ’s absorption of liquidity from deposit-taking institutions (DTIs) through open market operations to facilitate additional credit to the productive sector and gradual reductions in money market interest rates.  ￼

5. Quarterly Monetary Policy Report Press Conference – May 21, 2024
	•	Policy Decision: The MPC maintained the policy rate at 7.0%, emphasizing the need for inflation to be firmly anchored within the target range before considering rate reductions. Governor Byles highlighted the importance of patience as the BOJ navigated the inflationary environment.  ￼

Throughout these engagements, Governor Byles has consistently focused on achieving price stability, managing inflation expectations, and ensuring the effective transmission of monetary policy to support Jamaica’s economic objectives.
